The page has four elements with class `item`. What happens when this runs?

```js
const items = document.getElementsByClassName('item');
for (let i = 0; i < items.length; i++) {
  items[i].classList.remove('item');
}
```

What is logged when the user clicks the button?

```js
btn.addEventListener('click', (e) => {
  console.log('A');
  e.stopPropagation();
});
btn.addEventListener('click', () => console.log('B'));
parent.addEventListener('click', () => console.log('P'));
```

What does the last line log?

```js
const frag = document.createDocumentFragment();
for (let i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
  frag.append(document.createElement('li'));
}
list.append(frag);
console.log(frag.childNodes.length);
```

This loop widens 500 boxes and takes several hundred milliseconds. Why, and what is the fix?

```js
for (const box of boxes) {
  box.style.width = (box.offsetWidth + 10) + 'px';
}
```

Each list item looks like `<li data-id="7"><span class="title">Item</span></li>`. This delegated handler sometimes does nothing when items are clicked. Why, and what is the robust fix?

```js
list.addEventListener('click', (e) => {
  if (e.target.matches('li')) select(e.target.dataset.id);
});
```

